$DIR/main.p:16:7: cannot move s: it starts tasks at main.p:15, which find it at its address
    s is a sync.Scope, and s.Task at main.p:15 starts tasks that tell s when
    they return, at the address s has in this function. Moving s (assigning,
    passing or returning it) would leave them telling the old place, so nothing
    would wait for them where s ends up.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    Keep s where it is and wait for its tasks before it goes:
        s.Wait()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3405" for a longer explanation)
